[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) said one issue with one fix queued. Its own table already counted two
causes, and its footnote hid a third problem inside the bigger one. This page splits the ticket
into the problems it held, with one ticket each, and says where each one stands now.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but not everything in it is fixed. The one-day moves are still open.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Counts are the desk's own view of the export: the same rule as the table on #4471, one report id
counted once.

### `parser`: the shared parser read every date month first — fixed

A day-first tenant wrote 7 April, the parser read 4 July. Every report here that names two dates
names the same day and month swapped, except the one-day moves below. Engineering shipped
`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` and closed #4471 on 5 October.

### `hint`: booking-v3 printed the wrong hint under its date field — won't fix

The booking-v3 form showed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field and read the date the tenant's way,
day first. People typed what the hint said, so their dates swapped before the parser saw them.
Only reports sent through the form of a booking-v3 tenant land here. The parser fix does not touch
it; engineering retired the booking-v3 form instead, so no fix will come.

### `shift`: the date moves by one day — still open

These are not swaps. A Tuesday arrives as a Monday; a client in Lisbon gets Tuesday while a client
in Toronto gets Monday; one invite arrived before its booking existed. That points at a time zone
in the invite's date, not at the parser, so the parser fix does not close it, and retiring
booking-v3 does not either: none of these came through a form. The desk listed them in
`desk-notes.json` and counts them under `parser` by tenant; the table shows them in both places.
Nothing in a report's fields marks them, so this row is only as complete as that list: read the
body. If one-day moves keep coming after 5 October, this is why.

## Why intake could not split them

Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser locale. The cause depends on the tenant: where
they are and which booking form they run. The "tagged as" column shows how often the two
disagree. Merging on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) cannot
split them either: all three problems use the same handful of subjects.

## Lessons

- A big ticket can hold more than one bug. Closing it on one fix closes the others unfixed.
- Read the bodies. The cause is in the tenant and the dates, not in the subject or the browser.

